    <p>
      We can extend the definition to more than <m>3</m> events, but you need to
      check all pairs, triples, etc. when you extend it. If the context is
      clear, then we often just say <alert>independent</alert> instead of
      <alert>mutually independent</alert>. 
    </p>
</page>

<page>
<exercise>
<introduction>
    <p>
      The probability that my internet will fail (F) at my house on any given
      day is <m>P(F)=\frac{3}{4}</m>. If we assume that the probability of
      failure on any day is independent of any other day, find the following:
    </p>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         What is the probability that my internet will fail on Monday and
            Tuesday, but not on Wednesday or Thursday?
      </p>
    </statement>
</task>
      <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
          <p>
            What is the probability that my internet will fail at least one time
            in the next four days?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
          <p>
            What is the probability my internet will fail two of the next five
            days?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
</exercise> 
</page>

<page>
    <exercise workspace=".75in">
    <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose that <m>P(A)=0.45, P(B)=0.80,</m> and
            <m>P(A \cap B)=0.36</m>. Are <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> independent?
            Explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
<exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose that <m>P(A)=0.25, P(B)=0.40,</m> and
            <m>P(A \cup B)=0.45</m>. Are <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> independent?
            Explain.
          </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

