      </page>

      <page>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
             <p>
            A rocket has built-in redundant systems. In this system, if
            component <m>K_{1}</m> fails, then it is bypassed and component
            <m>K_{2}</m> is used. If component <m>K_{2}</m> fails, it is
            bypassed and component <m>K_{3}</m> is used. The system only fails
            if all of the components <m>K_{1}, K_{2},</m> and <m>K_{3}</m> fail.
            Suppose the probability that any given component is faulty (and
            would fail if needed) is <m>0.15</m> and assume that the failures of
            components are mutually independent events.
            </p>
            </introduction>
            <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                  What is the probability that one of the components is faulty
                  (but not the other 2)?
                </p>
              </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                  What is the probability that two of the components are faulty
                  (but not the third)?
                </p>
              </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
              <statement>
                <p>
                  What is the probability that whole the system fails?
                </p>
              </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
              <statement>
                <p>
                  Suppose instead there are 5 components (
                  <m>K_{1},K_{2}, K_{3}, K_{4}, K_{5})</m>. What is the
                  probability that exactly two are faulty?
                </p>
              </statement>
            </task>
          </exercise>
     </page>
     
      
    </worksheet>